
---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "@/components/nav.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/footer.astro";
---

<Layout title="Wazuh SIEM Home Lab | Alfayo Robin">
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-12 text-[var(--white)]">
    <a
      href="/#projects"
      class="mb-8 inline-block text-sm text-[var(--sec)] transition hover:opacity-80"
    >
      ← Back to Projects
    </a>

    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="mb-3 text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-[var(--sec)]">
        Cybersecurity • Security Operations • Home Lab
      </p>

      <h1 class="mb-5 text-3xl font-bold md:text-5xl">
        Wazuh SIEM Home Lab
      </h1>

      <p class="max-w-3xl text-base leading-7 opacity-80 md:text-lg">
        A hands-on security monitoring lab built with Wazuh, Kali Linux and
        Metasploitable 3 in VirtualBox. I configured connectivity, generated
        controlled security activity and observed the resulting alerts in Wazuh.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {["Wazuh SIEM", "VirtualBox", "Kali Linux", "Metasploitable 3", "Nmap", "Hydra", "Log Analysis"].map((item) => (
          <span class="rounded-full border border-white/20 px-3 py-1 text-sm">
            {item}
          </span>
        ))}
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">1. Project Overview</h2>
      <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
        The objective of this project was to gain practical experience with
        security monitoring and event detection using Wazuh. I set up the lab
        in VirtualBox, worked through network connectivity issues, connected
        the monitored environment and explored alerts generated during
        controlled testing.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-7 opacity-85">
        This exercise helped me understand how a security analyst can use a
        SIEM platform to identify suspicious activity, review event details
        and begin investigating potential security incidents.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">2. Lab Architecture</h2>

      <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-5">
          <h3 class="mb-2 font-semibold">Wazuh</h3>
          <p class="text-sm leading-6 opacity-80">
            Central security monitoring platform used to view the dashboard
            and review generated security alerts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-5">
          <h3 class="mb-2 font-semibold">Kali Linux</h3>
          <p class="text-sm leading-6 opacity-80">
            Testing machine used to generate controlled network scanning
            and security testing activity within the lab.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-5">
          <h3 class="mb-2 font-semibold">Metasploitable 3</h3>
          <p class="text-sm leading-6 opacity-80">
            Intentionally vulnerable target machine used for authorized
            security testing in the isolated virtual environment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-5">
          <h3 class="mb-2 font-semibold">Oracle VirtualBox</h3>
          <p class="text-sm leading-6 opacity-80">
            Virtualization platform used to run the lab machines and
            configure their virtual network connectivity.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-6 opacity-70">
        The exercises were performed in my own lab environment. Virtual
        machine network settings were configured to enable the required
        communication between the systems.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">3. Setup and Configuration</h2>

      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-4 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
        <li>
          <strong>Prepared the virtual environment.</strong>
          VirtualBox and Kali Linux were already installed, so I continued
          with the Wazuh setup and preparation of the Metasploitable 3 target.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Configured network connectivity.</strong>
          I checked the virtual machine network adapter settings and IP
          addresses while troubleshooting access to the Wazuh dashboard.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Verified communication.</strong>
          I used connectivity checks, including ping, to confirm communication
          between the lab machines where required.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Connected the monitored system.</strong>
          I confirmed that Metasploitable 3 appeared as a Wazuh agent and
          continued troubleshooting the monitoring setup.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Accessed the Wazuh dashboard.</strong>
          After resolving the initial connectivity issues, I accessed the
          dashboard and explored the available monitoring information.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">4. Detection Exercises</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-5">
          <h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">Nmap network scanning</h3>
          <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
            I performed an Nmap scan within the lab and observed security
            alerts in Wazuh associated with the scanning activity. This
            demonstrated how network reconnaissance can generate events
            that are useful for security monitoring.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 opacity-70">
            Analyst relevance: review the alert timestamp, affected agent,
            source information and rule details to understand what activity
            was detected.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-5">
          <h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">Hydra testing activity</h3>
          <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
            I also generated controlled Hydra activity in the lab and
            observed related security events in Wazuh. This provided exposure
            to monitoring activity that may be associated with repeated
            authentication attempts.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 opacity-70">
            Analyst relevance: examine the available event details and
            determine whether the activity indicates repeated login attempts,
            which account or service was involved, and whether further
            investigation is required.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-6 opacity-70">
        These exercises demonstrate alert visibility, not proof that every
        attempt was successful or that a complete incident investigation
        was performed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">5. Screenshots and Evidence</h2>

      <p class="mb-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
        The following screenshots are from the lab. They provide visual
        evidence of the Wazuh dashboard and the monitoring work carried
        out during testing.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <img
            src="/images/Projects/Wazuh/wazuh1.png"
            alt="Wazuh SIEM dashboard from the home lab"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg object-contain"
          />
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 opacity-75">
            <strong>Evidence 1 — Wazuh dashboard.</strong>
            The monitoring interface used to access the lab's security
            monitoring information.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <img
            src="/images/Projects/Wazuh/wazuh2.png"
            alt="Wazuh lab screenshot showing monitoring evidence"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg object-contain"
          />
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 opacity-75">
            <strong>Evidence 2 — Monitoring view.</strong>
            Additional visual evidence from the Wazuh lab. Review the
            displayed event or status details when examining this screenshot.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <img
            src="/images/Projects/Wazuh/wazuh3.png"
            alt="Additional Wazuh security monitoring evidence"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg object-contain"
          />
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 opacity-75">
            <strong>Evidence 3 — Security monitoring evidence.</strong>
            A further record of the monitoring activity carried out during
            the exercise.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-3">
          <img
            src="/images/Projects/Wazuh/wazuh4.png"
            alt="Additional screenshot from the Wazuh SIEM home lab"
            loading="lazy"
            class="w-full rounded-lg object-contain"
          />
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 opacity-75">
            <strong>Evidence 4 — Lab results.</strong>
            Additional evidence captured during the Wazuh setup and
            monitoring exercises.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-6 opacity-70">
        Before publishing, ensure each caption accurately describes what
        its screenshot shows. Avoid displaying passwords, tokens or other
        sensitive information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">6. Challenges and Troubleshooting</h2>

      <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
        One challenge was accessing the Wazuh dashboard through its assigned
        IP address. I investigated the VirtualBox network configuration and
        checked connectivity between the machines. I also encountered agent
        connectivity issues during the setup process and worked through the
        monitoring configuration before confirming that the lab was producing
        useful security events.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-4 leading-7 opacity-85">
        The experience reinforced the importance of checking network
        configuration, confirming agent status and distinguishing a
        connectivity problem from a lack of detected security activity.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">7. Skills Demonstrated</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 leading-7 opacity-85">
        <li>SIEM setup and dashboard navigation using Wazuh.</li>
        <li>Virtual machine and network connectivity troubleshooting.</li>
        <li>Security event monitoring and alert review.</li>
        <li>Exposure to network reconnaissance and authentication testing.</li>
        <li>Understanding alert context, severity and the need for investigation.</li>
        <li>Documenting lab configuration, observations and technical evidence.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="rounded-xl border border-white/15 p-6">
      <h2 class="mb-3 text-2xl font-semibold">8. Conclusion and Next Steps</h2>

      <p class="leading-7 opacity-85">
        This project gave me practical experience setting up a security
        monitoring lab and observing alerts associated with Nmap scanning
        and Hydra activity. I also developed my troubleshooting skills by
        resolving virtual network and agent connectivity issues. My next
        steps are to examine individual alerts in greater depth, document
        their rule IDs and severity, correlate relevant events, and practise
        writing concise SOC investigation reports.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
